#
# Zero-copy CPU tiles: painted straight into memory the GPU samples.
#
# With tiles painted on the CPU (pacific-env, PACIFIC_GPU_MODE=mali) each new
# tile was copied into a GPU texture on the compositor thread
# (glTexSubImage2D). As the phones that scrolled well on one core did, a
# whole tile is painted into a gralloc buffer (libhybris
# EGL_HYBRIS_native_buffer2, mapped cached for the CPU) from a pool, and the
# compositor shows it through that buffer's EGLImage: no upload, no copy.
# Buffers and their EGLImages are made once and reused (a buffer per tile
# cost the main thread a fifth of its time, importing each into the driver a
# third of the compositor's), 50 ms after the last frame that showed them. A
# later partial update is copied into the same buffer on the CPU. The pool
# holds at most a sixth of the open-files limit in buffers (384 at most): each
# costs about three descriptors, and at the limit (1024 on Sailfish) a long
# scroll left no descriptor for the next frame buffer and the page froze;
# tiles past it take the upload path. Partial
# and low-res paints, GPU painting and systems without libhybris keep the
# upload path. eglCreateImageKHR, not EGL 1.5's eglCreateImage: Mali
# crashes on images of native buffers made with the latter.
# PACIFIC_ZERO_COPY_TILES=0 turns it off.
#
